as we continue to spotlight a new book  each week during the shutdown we turn to  lincoln  on the verge the true story about what  may have been the most difficult moment  in this nation's history and the  extraordinary trip  directly in the middle of it author ted  whitmer details how our nation's 16th  president  survived those pivotal 13 days  november 1860 abraham lincoln is elected  in a four-way contest with just under 40  percent of the votes  but enough to make him the 16th  president storm clouds gathered for  months before lincoln was finally able  to set off for the white house  by train with everything on the line  when he left on this trip he just been  elected president one would think  he's pretty happy to be to be moving  forward and doing this he wasn't  it was the worst presidential transition  in our history for for him and really  for everyone because  it was anything but a joyful event his  election  immediately led to southern promises  that they would succeed and then they  start to do it ted whitmer's new book  lincoln on the verge details the chaos  hijinks deadly threats and narrow  escapes  during 13 days on the rails at the time  he makes this trip seven states have  already seceded  right more more to come more to come but  crucially  the ones they were the most worried  about did not secede  while he was on the train so marilyn  might have gone  and if marilyn goes there's no way he's  he can get to washington  the winding route that began in  springfield illinois  zigged and zagged across seven states  before eventually arriving  in d.c the railroad is behind a lot of  lincoln's career  and he actually loved railroads and he  was a railroad lawyer  and as i argue in the book the train is  a very  important symbol for him of progress  trains could go 60 70 miles an hour  lincoln is moving along pretty fast in  some parts of this  route and the cars he sat  in were quite plush he'd come a long way  from his log  cabin and i described these sort of  elegant sofas and these settees and the  paintings that were hanging in these you  know it's like an early air force one  that he's moving along on  and that was part of the allure people  not only wanted to see abraham lincoln  they wanted to see these very elegant  trains  the presidential train coming through  their small town  so it's not just lincoln that is  exciting to people but the idea of the  presidency itself which had become  pretty weak in the 1850s and after  lincoln's four years the presidency will  always be an important office the  biggest threat of the journey came  from an alleged plot to assassinate the  president-elect  in baltimore lincoln was never eager to  talk about it  but widmer argues in detail it was very  real  the miracle really is that it did not  succeed we all know  how lincoln's life ended in 1865 but i  i came away from the close study of the  assassination plot in 1861 with a  deep sense of gratitude that he made it  at all  whitmer says the plot to kill lincoln  before he could take office  was uncovered by dorothea dix who'd  gained fame for her work with a mentally  handicapped  she in effect became a spy rooting out  dangerous information alan pinkerton  of the famed pinkerton detective agency  and a team of his agents  were recruited to help foil the plot  it's pretty fascinating to hear about  it's a bit like a mission impossible  team they put on southern accents  and they quickly infiltrate the plot and  figure out  the details and that's how word gets to  lincoln that he's got to go through  baltimore in the middle of the night  which he does with alan pinkerton and  one friend  in an ordinary passenger seat at four in  the morning  but a potential assassination while the  gravest of the threats on this trip  was not the only one his son lost his  inaugural address multiple times  welcoming committees were firing cannons  toward the trains right the country's  falling apart  everything is going wrong and he he had  to keep his cool  sometimes his worst enemies were his  friends  sometimes when he came into a town all  of the windows in the train would  shatter because people fired the guns  too close to the train  or in one case a cannon was fired into  the train  so it's it's pretty haphazard he's  trying to  deal with all of that at the same time i  mean he's a normal  guy too he he's lincoln he has these  superhuman talents that are beginning to  be discovered  but he's also just trying to get his  family and his  difficult kids and his difficult wife  from stop to stop on a long train trip  it's a little bit like um a chevy chase  movie  good reference with chevy chase that  came out of nowhere  sounds about right now he kept it  together mentally  and he was always funny when he needs to  be i mean lincoln has a very quick wit  always served him well as a candidate  and as a president  but he's processing all of this deeply  and  thinking hard about a message that can  bring all these very different kinds of  people and one of the  main points of the book is he's seeing  rural people he's seeing city dwellers  he's seeing a lot of immigrants  and what is the special sauce that can  keep  all of these very different people  together and he's saying beautiful  things about what it means to be  american  lincoln's writing and speeches got  better and better  as he was able to effectively harness  and deliver the message he'd been  painstakingly trying to get across from  day one  that saving the republic as it stands  was the only option  for americans then and now i argue in  the end of the book that if the us  doesn't stay together the cause of  democracy  is fatally weakened it wasn't doing very  well  around the world when lincoln was  elected  but by saving american democracy i  believe he  saved the cause of democracy worldwide  and if the united states  hadn't been one powerful country in the  20th century  i think we would have seen a very  different final result in world war  ii and we'd be living in a very  different world today  that is quite something to think about  dana and at last count there were  15 about 15 000 books on lincoln not all  of them  are worth reading lincoln on the verge  is  well once you get a chevy chase  reference and then all i can think is  fletch follow play  and now abraham lincoln too great job  all right
 

